# The world answers v1 (chroma-world/spheres/world-answers-v1.md, 10-19), writer 3: faith, arts, gather.
# E4 the sphere asks (asked_help at rung 2, asked_lead at rung 3, asked_speak at rung 4), one of each a sphere, and
# F2 decisions for others, four a sphere. Read only with the Engine's world_answers switch on.
# Decision colours over this file: each colour leads 7 times (4 one-colour options, 3 crosses), and the 15 crosses are
# the cycles s1, s2 and s3 once each, so the count is even whether a cross counts for its first colour or for both.

# ================================================================ E4, faith

== the elders ask for Saturday mornings at the shared meal
tier: everyday
sphere: faith
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-85
calls: WG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, faith
horizon: months
roles: elder, regular, friend
cast_want: asked_help
earth: you have gone to your congregation for some years, and the elders ask whether you could give your Saturday mornings to the meal it serves for anyone who comes
gap: 3-8
scene: For years {N} has sat in the same row at {place}, come for the holy days and stayed for the tea after. This week one of the elders takes {N} aside by the coat pegs. The shared meal on Saturday mornings feeds forty people from the street, the cook is getting old, and they need someone steady to help. "Only if it suits," the elder says, and means it.
scene W: Forty people come to that meal every week, and the work of it falls on fewer hands each year.
scene U: {N} has watched how the kitchen runs, and can see how much better it could run.
scene B: Being asked means {N} has been noticed, and the ones who run the meal are the ones who run {place}.
scene R: Saturday morning is the one slow morning in {Ns} week, and it is precious.
scene G: The meal is older than anyone who serves it, and the ones who keep it going are a family of a kind.
- W | take a fixed Saturday on the rota, every week, without fail | v: benevolence, conformity | lever: loyalty | foot: place faith.congregation | chance: 85
- U | help for a month, then rework how the kitchen orders and stores its food | v: achievement, universalism | lever: voice | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | say yes, and ask for a seat on the committee that runs the meal | v: power, achievement | lever: lead | foot: person elder | chance: 65
- R | come when the mood is right, and bring a guitar for the people in the queue | v: stimulation, benevolence | lever: voice | foot: place arts.song | chance: 75
- G | cook beside the old cook and learn the dishes the meal has always served | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: loyalty | foot: person mentor | chance: 85
- W>U | offer to keep the meal's rota and accounts, so nobody is left alone at the stove | v: conformity, universalism | lever: loyalty | foot: means time-.06 | chance: 80
- U>B | find a grocer who will give the day's leftovers, in return for public thanks | v: achievement, power | lever: fund | foot: person patron | chance: 65
- B>R | keep the Saturdays, but offer to run one big fund-raising night instead | v: power, stimulation | lever: fund | foot: place gather.great | chance: 70
- R>G | say yes on the spot, and talk a friend into coming too | v: benevolence, hedonism | lever: loyalty | foot: person friend | chance: 80
- G>W | say gently that the family needs Saturdays, and keep coming as before | v: tradition, security | lever: neglect | foot: means time+.03 | chance: 90
works: By spring {N} knows the regulars at the meal by name, and the elders speak of {N} warmly whatever the answer was.
works: The arrangement {N} settles on holds, and the Saturday meal goes on without anyone being worn thin.
fails: The arrangement {N} settles on does not hold, and by the next season the elders are asking around again.
fails: {N} misjudges what the answer would cost, and the weeks after are more tiring than anyone expected.

== the young people's group needs someone to lead it
tier: everyday
sphere: faith
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-80
calls: GR
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, faith
horizon: years
roles: elder, regular, colleague
cast_want: asked_lead
earth: the leader of your congregation's young people's group is moving away, and the elders ask whether you would take it on
gap: 3-8
scene: The young people's group at {place} meets on the evening after the holy day, a dozen of them between twelve and eighteen, and its leader is moving away. The elders have watched {N} for years now, and they ask whether {N} would take it on: the meetings, the summer trip, the questions nobody else wants to answer.
scene W: The young ones need someone reliable, the same face every week, who keeps the ways of the house.
scene U: Half the group's questions are hard ones, and {N} would want to answer them honestly or not at all.
scene B: Whoever shapes the young ones shapes what {place} will be in twenty years.
scene R: {N} remembers being fourteen and bored on a hard bench, and would want these evenings to be alive.
scene G: The group has its old songs and its summer camp by the lake; someone has to carry them on.
- W | take it on, with house rules agreed with the parents from the start | v: conformity, security | lever: lead | lead: rules | foot: place faith.congregation | chance: 75
- U | take it on for hard questions, reading the old texts side by side | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: lead | lead: knowing | foot: place faith.seeking | chance: 70
- B | agree, on condition that the group gets a room and a budget of its own | v: power, achievement | lever: voice | foot: person elder | chance: 65
- R | say yes, and fill the evenings with music, cooking and long walks | v: stimulation, hedonism | lever: lead | lead: inspiring | foot: means time-.06 | chance: 70
- G | leave the leading to another, but keep helping with the summer camp | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: loyalty | foot: person regular | chance: 85
- W>B | lead it for one year, with a successor named from the start | v: conformity, achievement | lever: lead | lead: rules | foot: door kept your word | chance: 75
- U>R | say no to leading, but offer one evening a month of open questions | v: self-direction, stimulation | lever: voice | foot: means time+.03 | chance: 80
- B>G | suggest a younger member for the post, and offer to stand behind them | v: power, tradition | lever: voice | foot: person friend | chance: 75
- R>W | say honestly that a reluctant leader would waste the young ones' evenings | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: exit | foot: door turned down a chance | chance: 90
- G>U | ask the old leader to stay one more season and teach the ways first | v: tradition, achievement | lever: loyalty | foot: person mentor | chance: 70
works: The group meets on through the year, and a few of the young ones start staying behind to talk with {N} afterwards.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the elders see it was thought through, and the group is in steady hands by the summer.
fails: The first months are harder than {N} thought, and twice the parents come to the elders with worries.
fails: The arrangement falls between two stools, and the group meets less often than it did before.

== the council wants a voice from the congregation
tier: everyday
sphere: faith
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-90
calls: WU
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, faith
horizon: months
roles: elder, neighbour, regular
cast_want: asked_speak
earth: the town council will decide the fate of the old burial ground by the river, and the elders ask you to speak for the congregation at the hearing
gap: 4-10
scene: The town council means to sell the old burial ground by the river, where the families of {place} have laid their dead for two hundred years, and it is holding a hearing first. It wants someone to speak for the congregation, and the elders ask {N}, a name people in {sphere_name} know. The hearing is in three weeks, with the local paper watching and the buyers in the front row.
scene W: The congregation has a right to be heard, and a duty to speak for the whole town, not only for itself.
scene U: {N} would want the history of that ground, its deeds and its maps, before standing up to say a word.
scene B: A speech before the council is a chance to become the face of {place} in town.
scene R: {N} feels strongly about that ground, and fears the feeling would show in every word.
scene G: People's grandparents lie in that ground; whoever speaks, speaks for them too.
- W | agree, and first ask the members what the congregation wants said | v: universalism, conformity | lever: lead | lead: rules | foot: place rule.voice | chance: 75
- U | agree, and spend the three weeks in the archive with the old deeds and maps | v: achievement, universalism | lever: voice | foot: place learn.keeping | chance: 75
- B | agree, and meet the council members one by one before the hearing | v: power, achievement | lever: lead | lead: favours | foot: person go-between | chance: 65
- R | agree, and read aloud the names of those buried there | v: benevolence, stimulation | lever: voice | foot: person rival | chance: 60
- G | stand with the families, and let the eldest of them speak instead | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: loyalty | foot: person elder | chance: 80
- W>R | agree, on the condition that the young members speak alongside | v: universalism, self-direction | lever: lead | lead: inspiring | foot: person colleague | chance: 70
- U>G | write a careful statement for the hearing, and let another read it | v: tradition, achievement | lever: voice | foot: lesson a written case travels far | chance: 80
- B>W | say yes only with a free hand over what is said | v: power, self-direction | lever: voice | foot: lesson a free hand has a price | chance: 65
- R>U | decline, saying plainly that it is too close to speak about calmly | v: self-direction, security | lever: exit | foot: door turned down a chance | chance: 90
- G>B | put forward the head of the oldest family, and quietly back them | v: tradition, power | lever: loyalty | foot: person neighbour | chance: 75
works: The hearing is put off for a year, and people in town say the congregation's case was put well.
works: Whoever stood up for {place}, the families feel they were heard, and some of them thank {N} after.
fails: The council hears the case politely and carries on, and the sale goes ahead in the spring.
fails: The hearing goes badly, and for a while {place} is talked about in town more than it would like.

