roles: elder, regular, neighbour
earth: the congregation must decide whether its rites stay in the old tongue or move to the tongue people speak at home, and {N}'s word now carries
scene: For as long as anyone remembers, the rites at {place} have been said in the old tongue. The young ones follow the sounds and not the meaning, and the newcomers follow neither. The elders have argued it for a year, and now they look to {N}.
- G | keep the rites in the old tongue, as they have always been said | v: tradition | scope: place | lever: loyalty | chance: 75
- W | keep the old tongue on holy days, and the home tongue on all the others | v: conformity, universalism | scope: place | lever: lead | chance: 70
- U>R | put the rites into the home tongue, so each can follow and judge for themselves | v: self-direction, universalism | scope: town_sphere | lever: voice | chance: 60
works: Within a year the new way at {place} feels like the old one, and other congregations in town come to see how it is done.
works: The elders who argued longest are the first to say it was decided well.
fails: A handful of families leave for a congregation across town, and the rows at {place} are thinner for it.
fails: The change is half made and half resisted, and for a while every rite is an argument.

== what the house of worship does with its empty hall
tier: decision
cast_want: decide
sphere: faith
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-90
tone: mixed
life: faith, community
horizon: years
roles: elder, neighbour, colleague
earth: the congregation's old hall stands empty most of the week, and {N} must help decide what it becomes
scene: The hall behind {place} was built for a congregation twice the size. Now it stands empty six days in seven, the roof leaks, and the heating costs more each winter. Three plans are on the table, and the elders want {Ns} word before they vote.
- R | hand the hall to the young people for music, dances and their own nights | v: stimulation, hedonism | scope: place | lever: found | chance: 65
- U | turn it into a reading room and study hall, open to anyone in town | v: universalism, self-direction | scope: place | lever: lead | chance: 70
- B>G | rent it out to local businesses, and let the money keep the old building standing | v: security, tradition | scope: place | lever: fund | chance: 75
- W | open the hall to the night shelter at once, and sort out the council's permits afterwards | v: benevolence, universalism | scope: place | lever: subvert | chance: 60
works: Within a season the hall is full on most evenings, and people who never came to {place} now know where it is.
works: The roof is mended before the winter, and the plan that was chosen turns out to suit the street.
fails: The plan costs more than anyone counted, and the elders have to come back to it within a year.
fails: Some members feel the hall has been given away, and they say so at every meeting.

== whether to marry couples from two faiths
tier: decision
cast_want: decide
sphere: faith
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-90
tone: mixed
life: faith, family
horizon: years
roles: elder, regular, neighbour
earth: couples from two faiths have asked to be married at the house of worship, and {N}'s word decides the congregation's answer
scene: Three couples this year have asked to be married at {place}, each of them with one partner from another faith. The elders are split. Some say the house must stay what it is; some say a congregation that turns away love will empty. They have asked {N} to settle it.
- R>W | welcome every couple who asks, with a rite written for the two of them | v: benevolence, universalism | scope: town_sphere | lever: voice | chance: 60
- G>U | marry them in the old rite, once the couple has learned what it means | v: tradition, achievement | scope: place | lever: loyalty | chance: 70
- B | welcome them, and let the new families and their gifts grow the congregation | v: achievement, power | scope: place | lever: lead | chance: 70
works: The first of the weddings fills {place}, and both families stay on for the meal.
works: Other congregations in town hear how {place} decided, and some of them follow.
fails: Two elders step back from their duties in protest, and the house is quieter for a season.
fails: The answer pleases nobody fully, and the couples and the elders both feel half heard.

== the reform on who may be buried with their family
tier: decision
cast_want: decide
sphere: faith
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-90
tone: mixed
life: faith, family
horizon: years
roles: elder, colleague, rival
earth: the congregations of the whole country meet to vote on whether those who left the faith may still be buried beside their families, and {N} is sent to speak
scene: For generations, one who left the faith could not be laid in its ground beside their family. Now the congregations of the whole country are gathering to vote on a reform, and {place} sends {N}, whose name is known beyond the town. The hall is full of elders who have argued this for decades.
- W | write one rule for every congregation: all families may bury their dead together | v: universalism, benevolence | scope: nation | lever: voice | chance: 45
- G | leave it to each congregation's elders, as it has always been | v: tradition, security | scope: town_sphere | lever: loyalty | chance: 70
- B>W | back the reform in return for a seat on the council that writes the rules | v: power, universalism | scope: town_sphere | lever: lead | chance: 55
works: The vote goes the way {N} spoke, and within a few years the families who were kept apart lie together.
works: The gathering ends with an agreement most can live with, and the town's own congregations take it up.
fails: The vote is put off for another five years, and the old rule stands.
fails: The reform passes in words but is kept by few, and the argument goes on in every town.

